// C1.7 / ADR 0016 D6b over ADR 0015 D11, ADR 0045 A22, register D39: a NULLABLE whose
// payload is a generic-struct INSTANCE. `?Struct` heap-indirects to `{ i1, ptr }` so a
// recursive struct has a finite layout (ADR 0015 D11); ADR 0016 D6b added
// `GenericInstance` to `NullableInner`, so `?Node<i64>` must indirect for the same
// reason, and ADR 0045 A22 prescribes exactly that for `scg`'s `cgo_ty`/`ll_type_to`.
// `scg` implemented the struct half only, in all three of its places at once, and with
// THIS file's field order emitted `%Node_i64 = type { i64, { i1, %Node_i64 } }` —
// self-referential, and `llvm-as`: "Cannot allocate unsized type".
//
// ⚠ THE REGISTER FILED THIS AS A FIELD DEFECT ("a nullable generic-instance FIELD").
// It is every position where a `?GI` TYPE or `null` CONSTANT is rendered, which is why
// this file pins three sites in one program:
//   * `ll_type_to`   — the Pass-0 aggregate `%Node_i64 = type { i64, { i1, ptr } }`;
//   * `cgo_ty`       — the `next` local's `alloca` / `load` / `extractvalue { i1, ptr }`;
//   * `cgo_operand`  — the `null` constant `{ i1 0, ptr null }`.
// The `next` local is what separates the first two from the third: a `null` renders its
// type and its constant ADJACENTLY, so a struct-literal field alone cannot show that the
// type renderer and the operand renderer are independent decisions. The local supplies
// the constant-free `alloca`/`load`/`extractvalue` lines that only `cgo_ty` produces.
//
// ⚠ A FOURTH `?GI` RENDERER IS DELIBERATELY LEFT BROKEN: `cg_widen`
// (`selfhost/types/mir_emit.sentinel`) still renders a widen payload by value, so
// `let o: ?Bx<i64> = b;` emits `insertvalue { i1, ptr } …, %Bx_i64 %v2, 1`. The ORACLE
// refuses every such program ("widen-to-nullable of a struct/generic payload (heap-box)
// deferred"), so the differential skips it before `scg` runs and no fixture can reach
// it — `scg` itself cannot refuse (register D38), so that oracle refusal is the only
// thing keeping invalid IR out. inkwell's half of the widen IS fixed (register D42,
// pinned by `c17_nullable_generic_instance_widen.sentinel`); see `cg_widen`'s comment
// for what un-deferring the oracle requires first.
//
// ⚠ ANTI-REGRESSION — AND THE TWO GUARDS PIN OPPOSITE BRANCHES, which is the point:
//   * `tests/pass/c16_linked_list_node.sentinel` is the ONLY program in the whole
//     oracle-emitting corpus that pins the `?Struct` (heap-indirect) branch — measured:
//     it is the sole source of `{ i1 0, ptr null }` outside this file. A "fix" that
//     REPLACED the struct test with the generic-instance one would leave this file green
//     and turn c16 red.
//   * `tests/pass/c15_nullable_struct_field.sentinel` pins the FALL-THROUGH branch. Its
//     name means "a nullable field inside a struct", not "a nullable of a struct" — its
//     payload is `?i64`, and it emits `{ i1, i64 }` six times and no pointer at all. It
//     is the guard against the predicate being WIDENED, which is the failure the helper's
//     own ⚠ paragraph calls load-bearing.
// Both guards are needed and neither substitutes for the other.
//
// ⚠ The exit code is insensitive: nothing here reads the payload, so 42 does not depend
// on the lowering. (It is not "42 under the broken lowering" either — `run_exit` builds
// through inkwell, which was never on the broken path, and the pre-fix `scg` IR does not
// assemble, so it has no exit code at all.) The real pin is the codegen differential,
// which sweeps `tests/pass` and compares emitted IR byte-for-byte against `snc llvm`.
//
// 40 + 2 = 42.
struct Node<T> { v: T, next: ?Node<T> }

fn main() -> i64 {
    let head: Node<i64> = Node { v: 40, next: null };
    let next: ?Node<i64> = null;
    if is_some(next) { 1 } else { head.v + 2 }
}
